\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Schematic of a wind-driven vehicle's operation. Arrows indicate the direction of rotation. The pitch of the propeller blades is indicated by the darker shade of the blade facing the rear. (a) For downwind travel faster than the wind, the wheels drive the propeller, which pushes air backward to generate thrust. (b) For upwind travel, the wind turns the propeller (acting as a turbine), which drives the wheels and pulls the vehicle against the wind.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:vehicle_operation}{{1}{4}{Schematic of a wind-driven vehicle's operation. Arrows indicate the direction of rotation. The pitch of the propeller blades is indicated by the darker shade of the blade facing the rear. (a) For downwind travel faster than the wind, the wheels drive the propeller, which pushes air backward to generate thrust. (b) For upwind travel, the wind turns the propeller (acting as a turbine), which drives the wheels and pulls the vehicle against the wind}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Minimal kinematic definitions for Sec.~\ref {sec:water-sail-prop}. In the laboratory frame \(G\), the true wind is \(v_w\) and the boat speed is \(v\). In the vehicle frame \(C\), the apparent-wind velocity is \(v_a = v_w - v\). Vectors are aligned at a common origin; for \(v > v_w\), the apparent-wind velocity \(v_a\) reverses direction.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:water-frame}{{2}{4}{Minimal kinematic definitions for Sec.~\ref {sec:water-sail-prop}. In the laboratory frame \(G\), the true wind is \(v_w\) and the boat speed is \(v\). In the vehicle frame \(C\), the apparent-wind velocity is \(v_a = v_w - v\). Vectors are aligned at a common origin; for \(v > v_w\), the apparent-wind velocity \(v_a\) reverses direction}{figure.2}{}}
